WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.940
Today, we're going to be speaking to John
Mark Hansen, who's a professor in political

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.890
science at the University of Chicago.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:12.470
We are going to talk a little bit about what
would it look like to rig and or steal an

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.480
American presidential election.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.480
Mark, it's great to have you on.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.480
Thank you.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:18.480
It's great to be here.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.480
So, OK.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.410
I mean, at this point, I don't think we have
to rehash all of the claims that were made

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:28.609
between November 4th and January 6th or January
20th or even up until today and continue to

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:29.880
be made.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.310
Let's just get into the reality.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.920
So so maybe the audience can understand what
would go into this.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.250
Could could an American presidential election,
which is really 50 state elections, could

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.250
it be rigged?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.250
Could it be stolen?

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.559
How would it have to be done if so?

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:49.539
Well, I would say that it's difficult to the
point of impossible, I think many people just

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:56.089
don't understand the scale of the effort that
would be involved to actually fix a presidential

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.089
election.

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:59.420
You're not talking about a few hundred votes.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.999
You're not talking about a few thousand votes.

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:05.990
You're talking about tens or even hundreds
of thousands of votes spread along a number

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.380
of states.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:13.470
So there are folks who will say, well, you
know, 80 million votes versus 75 million or

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.470
whatever.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.979
You don't really need to flip millions of
votes.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:17.979
You really need.

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.159
For example, in 2016, it was 77000 votes in
three states.

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:26.220
In twenty twenty it was depending on how you
slice it, about 100000 votes.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.659
That's very different than million.

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:30.710
So that presumably would make it simpler.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:39.259
Simpler, but not by any means simple, if you
imagine what it would take to shift a number

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:43.950
of votes and bear in mind that you don't know
exactly where the problem is going to be,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.020
you don't know exactly what the margin you
need is and you don't know exactly where it's

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.350
going to be.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.289
So you have to start months in advance and
you have to be thinking in terms of tens of

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:59.359
thousands of votes and you have to be thinking
about how it is that you would recruit tens

00:01:59.359 --> 00:02:05.049
of thousands of people to participate in this
effort without anybody blowing the whistle

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:06.049
on you.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:08.070
OK, so let me play devil's advocate.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:13.070
Maybe you don't need to recruit tens of thousands
if you can just get a few tech people who,

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.280
quote, control the voting machines, that might
be a simple way.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.140
And you might only need five or six people.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:26.690
Well, that is the case, however, you still
have to maintain a conspiracy of those five

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.670
or six people, and one of the things we know
is it's very difficult to maintain conspiracies.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.980
So there are many, many business frauds that
go awry or criminal frauds or criminal activities

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.520
that go awry because people feel the need
to say something either because they want

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.090
to boast about it or because they have a guilty
conscience or whatever.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:52.420
So you would have to you would have to have
a great deal of trust in your Confederate's.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.480
But even beyond that, there are lots and lots
and lots of barriers have been erected to

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.790
actually manipulating the vote in that way.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:07.340
There are security measures for the technology
that's used in voting and vote counting.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:13.510
And moreover, there's the knowledge of the
there's a knowledge of the people who are

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.970
overseeing the voting process.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:19.090
And so at some county comes in all of a sudden
with ten thousand more votes than they've

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:24.190
ever cast before, someone might say, boy,
that looks kind of fishy.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.990
Maybe we ought to look closer at that.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:33.710
There are so many different claims that have
been made about mechanisms for subterfuge,

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.620
some of which conflict with each other.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.200
And maybe I don't know how much we need to
necessarily get into that.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.520
But just to talk about a few of the different
things, there's, as I mentioned, the idea

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:51.860
of simply using hacking skills to change the
vote tallies of a group of voting machines.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:56.871
We heard stories of so-called massive dumps
of ballots coming in in the middle of the

00:03:56.871 --> 00:04:03.330
night on vans and literally just taking boxes
of ballots out, which presumably in that case,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:08.720
the voting machines aren't being manipulated,
but someone's feeding in a bunch of ballots.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:16.100
There's the idea of having election workers
look at ballots and then disqualify only some,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.370
presumably to help one candidate and hurt
the other.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:22.340
All of these are based on different principles.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.430
And what to me seems very difficult would
be that you might end up with one of these

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:30.729
strategies working against the different one.

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:34.969
Conflicting the coordination of such strategies
seems extraordinarily difficult.

00:04:34.969 --> 00:04:38.770
Yeah, it's it's enormously difficult.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.849
Take take, for instance, the idea of a bunch
of ballots that get dumped.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:49.710
So most states, most jurisdictions, in fact,
allow partizan observers to ballot counting.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.860
And so all of a sudden these ballots come
in and someone might say, so where do they

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.550
come from?

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.139
How do we know that they're authentic?

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:59.539
There should be a mark on the ballot.

00:04:59.539 --> 00:05:05.699
There should be some kind of record that the
person at the polling place actually authorize

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:10.720
this person to vote or if it's an absentee
ballot, should meet the requirements for absentee

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.930
ballots, which oftentimes include a signature.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.349
They include a ballot application, all kinds
of things.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:21.889
So there are all kinds of safeguards to prevent
ballots from being submitted fraudulently

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:28.310
and certainly all kinds of safeguards to make
sure that if there are frauds, there's a likelihood

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.671
of their being discovered, particularly on
a large scale.

00:05:31.671 --> 00:05:37.150
Yeah, when it comes to in-person voter fraud,
the idea of someone I don't even know, sometimes

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.280
it's not even clear to me exactly how you
would do it.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.389
You would go to multiple polling places or
show up and claim to be a variety of different

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:44.389
people.

00:05:44.389 --> 00:05:46.069
I mean, you wouldn't be registered in multiple
places.

00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:48.699
So going to polling places as you wouldn't
wouldn't work.

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:52.970
I guess you could show up and say at one polling
place you say you're Joe Smith, and at the

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.870
next play you say you say you're Steve Johnson
and that type of thing.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:02.660
But it seems like in order to flip 50000 votes
with that strategy, you need thousands of

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:07.529
people making it incredibly unlikely to be
able to do it secretly.

00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:08.529
Exactly.

00:06:08.529 --> 00:06:09.529
Exactly.

00:06:09.529 --> 00:06:12.240
Just think about recruiting such a number
of people.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.120
So somehow you're going to have to find a
bunch of people who are willing to take the

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:24.389
risk either to their freedom or to their pocketbook
or to their reputations to participate in

00:06:24.389 --> 00:06:25.389
all of this.

00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:28.720
And you're going to have to make sure that
you don't reach anybody who has a healthy

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.539
enough conscience that they would blow the
whistle on you when it comes to people getting

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:40.759
caught attempting to do these things from
looking at the Brennan Center for Justice

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:43.599
work on this, which is looked at over a billion
votes over.

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:46.960
I don't I don't know how many years, many,
many years.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.090
They say that this in-person voter fraud is
not only extremely rare, but there is not

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:58.050
even a single case in which it has come close
to actually affecting the outcome of of a

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.590
race.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:06.779
There are people who will claim, maybe cynically,
maybe not, that just because the Brennan Center

00:07:06.779 --> 00:07:10.599
gives us these numbers, it doesn't address
the number of people that are getting away

00:07:10.599 --> 00:07:12.580
with it, which simply don't show up.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.360
When you hear about, oh, 20 people were caught
trying to do this, how can we defeat the idea

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.240
that maybe there are lots of people not caught?

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:21.240
Doing it.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:29.379
Well, again, I think the you have to kind
of do the math on it, so any kind of large

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:34.729
scale effort means that the difficulties go
up, the costs go up and the risk of exposure

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:35.729
goes up.

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:39.340
The more people who are involved in this,
the more votes that are involved in this,

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.529
the greater the likelihood that it's going
to be found.

00:07:41.529 --> 00:07:45.169
So, yeah, maybe there are people who get away
with it, but there are people who get away

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:48.729
with it, maybe affecting one or two votes.

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:53.759
You know, out of all the scrutiny that this
last election got so far, the only indictment

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:58.689
that we've seen for any kind of voting irregularities
was a man in Pennsylvania who voted an absentee

00:07:58.689 --> 00:07:59.870
ballot for his mother.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:00.870
Right.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:01.870
For Donald Trump.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:08.879
So that's that's the typical kind of thing
that we see in these cases, most cases of

00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:13.840
voter fraud or alleged voter fraud or not
even prosecuted because it's people making

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.249
honest mistakes.

00:08:15.249 --> 00:08:18.900
They thought that they were registered, but
they weren't where they thought that they

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.289
were eligible in this jurisdiction, but they
weren't or so forth.

00:08:22.289 --> 00:08:27.460
So most of these cases have quite innocent
explanations.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.820
What would be of all of these different mechanisms?

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:34.390
What's the one you're most likely to get away
with?

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:42.700
Oh, boy, I'm not sure that there are that
many possibilities anymore, you know, back

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.440
in the bad old days, there used to be a fair
bit of vote for it was committed both by Republicans

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.210
and by Democrats.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:57.630
And as a result of that, election, officials
learned through time what those what those

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.340
frauds look like.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:03.190
And they learned about how to take security
measures to make sure that those frauds didn't

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.570
occur.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:09.700
And so by now, with a century of experience
with the Australian ballot, with the secret

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:13.830
ballot, we have lots and lots of experience
in this.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:19.200
And it's not like people have just been twiddling
their thumbs and saying, oh, I when they encounter

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.190
problems, they've taken steps to avoid them
in the future.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:28.000
When it comes to the U.S. having these 50
election systems plus D.C., I guess you'd

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.440
say, you know, it's fifty one election systems.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:36.620
One could make the argument that the election
is less secure because you have so many different

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:37.830
systems.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:41.160
But on the other hand, you could make the
argument that having all of these systems

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.810
actually makes it more secure, because in
order to really do some kind of fraud at a

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.900
at a big level, you'd have to figure out a
way to manipulate multiple different systems.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:56.080
Do you think that having the 51 different
systems on balance makes the election more

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:57.460
or less secure?

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.390
That's a good question.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:02.240
I think you could argue it both ways.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.260
Certainly one could argue that if you have
separate systems, you might not have all jurisdictions

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:13.280
adopting best practices, and that would be
an argument for a more uniform system.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:19.570
On the other hand, if it is a matter of some
kind of technical expertize to manipulate

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:24.510
these systems, then having a wide variety
of systems means that you have to have a greater

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:28.790
variety of technical expertize in order to
do anything significant.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.930
Do you think that because of all of the the
smears that we've heard over the last two

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:39.720
or three months about the election of twenty
twenty, there's going to be a skepticism about

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.680
the system in some parts among some communities
that will last a long time?

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:50.080
Or is it as simple as if in twenty twenty
two politicians don't make these claims, people

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.620
will, generally speaking, trust the systems
again.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:58.840
Well, I think that there is now a partizan
reason not to, quote unquote trust the system,

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:03.130
whether that actually translates into people
behaving in a different way, I think is another

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.130
question entirely.

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:11.110
There are all these concerns in Georgia, for
instance, in the Senate runoff that the Donald

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:17.090
Trump's casting aspersions on the fairness
of the Georgia election would mean that Republicans

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.030
would stay home.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.670
Why right.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:23.190
Then they definitely lose.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.190
Right.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:25.190
Right.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.190
Why would you do that?

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:30.520
So so I'm skeptical that it will matter a
whole lot.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:37.441
What it what it does do, however, is to raise
the possibility of just casting doubt on the

00:11:37.441 --> 00:11:39.090
legitimacy of every election outcome.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:40.470
And that's a bad thing.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:41.470
Yeah.

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:46.210
And that it seems I mean, I there was a woman
who confronted Mitt Romney in an airport a

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.290
couple of weeks ago.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:53.740
And almost like as a reflexive thing, she
said you weren't your election wasn't even

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:54.740
legitimate.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:55.740
And like who?

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.660
I didn't even know the Utah twenty eighteen
election was being challenged in terms of

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:00.380
its legitimate legitimacy.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.080
It seems it's becoming a catchall for.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.760
I don't like that you won.

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:05.710
Yeah, that's right.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:06.710
That's right.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.250
And this has been brewing for a while.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:14.220
It's been a Republican strategy since 2010,
basically to elect more, to erect more and

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:24.380
more and more barriers to the voting and particularly
to the constituencies that tend to vote Democratic

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:27.820
just in order to keep one's opponents away
from the polls.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:28.820
Right.

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:34.220
So, you know, this is actually and in that
respect, the twenty twenty election was something

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:40.010
of an embarrassment because we had easier
access to voting than we've ever had before

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:41.640
in our history.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.480
And we have a whistle clean election.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:46.220
So now what are they going to claim?

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:47.220
Right.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:51.630
How how can they how can they say, well, actually,
things do work.

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.970
And so we get a doubling down on these fraud
claims.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:54.970
Yeah.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.880
And in fact, Christopher Krebs, who worked
for Donald Trump, said exactly that.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:00.760
It was fine and he was fired.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:04.430
And so we see the political nature that it's
taking on.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:08.920
We've been speaking with Professor John Mark
Hanson, who teaches political science at the

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.690
University of Chicago.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:11.720
So great having you on today.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:13.090
I really appreciate it.

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:14.090
It's been fun.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:14.360
Thank you.

